  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121958,
      "SummaryText": "The purpose of the Whole Foods Market Process Safety Management Program is to assist with the prevention of accidental releases of toxic substances that can cause harm to the public, facility employees, and to the environment.\nThe Whole Foods Process Safety Management Program is designed to comply with the OSHA regulations (29 CFR 1910.119), and the Environmental Protection Agency regulations in 40 CFR Part 68.  The stationary source is the anhydrous ammonia refrigeration system located at the Whole Foods Distribution Center 400 E. Johnson Ave., Cheshire, CT.  Whole Foods has created both the PSM and the Risk Management Program as part of their dedication to the health and safety of their employees, as well as the additional community and public receptors.  The only regulated substance at this facility is anhydrous ammonia, which is classified as a toxic substance, liquefied under pressure.  \nThis facility is a 155,000 sq.ft. warehousing distribution center with cold storage capabilities situated on an approximately 40 acre industrially zoned parcel.  The refrigeration system encompasses three separate ammonia machinery rooms and has a combined inventory of approximately 16,198 pounds of anhydrous ammonia stored on site.  This process meets the requirements of EPA's Program 3.\nThis PSM Manual and RMP had been developed by HCG Associates, LLC on behalf of the Whole Foods Distribution Center, specifically to prevent or mitigate accidental releases associated with the storage and use of anhydrous ammonia at the Cheshire facility.  These programs were developed to meet the requirements of both the OSHA PSM, and the RMP regulations and is available to government agencies and the public for review.\n\nThe PSM and RMP programs specifically address the following areas:\n\nAccidental release prevention and emergency response: Third party refrigeration contractor provides required inspections, testing, and calibration of refrigeration equipment including sensors. Facility has dev"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000121958,
      "SummaryText": "eloped an Emergency Action Plan and relies on the local fire department and HAZMAT teams for ammonia emergency response.\n\nChemical specific prevention: Only qualified anhydrous ammonia refrigeration technicians operate the refrigeration system. Ammonia awareness training is provided to all employees.\nThere have been no accidental releases within the past 5-year period.\n\nPlanned changes to improve safety; Non-destructive Testing on refrigeration piping and equipment has recently been performed.\n\n  The Whole Foods PSM/RMP program consists of the following 15 elements:\n\u00bf\tIntroduction\n\u00bf\tEmployee Participation\n\u00bf\tProcess Safety Information\n\u00bf\tProcess Hazard Analysis\n\u00bf\tOperating Procedures\n\u00bf\tTraining\n\u00bf\tContractor Qualifications\n\u00bf\tPre-Start Up Safety Review\n\u00bf\tMechanical Integrity\n\u00bf\tHot Work Permit\n\u00bf\tManagement of Change\n\u00bf\tIncident Investigation\n\u00bf\tEmergency Planning and Response\n\u00bf\tCompliance Audits\n\u00bf\tTrade Secrets"
    }
  ]
